.TH INTRO 1
.SH NAME
intro \- introduction to the commands of the Development System
.SH DESCRIPTION
This section of the manual describes the commands of the xenix86-devsys
Development System, which adds to XENIX System V 2.1.3 for the 8086 and
8088 what is needed to write programs in C and in assembly language:
.TP 10
.B cc
compile C programs
.TP
.B as86
assemble 8086 assembly language
.TP
.B ld86
link object files into a program (cc runs it)
.TP
.B ar86
make and change libraries of object files
.TP
.B make
keep the files of a program up to date
.TP
.B nm
print the symbols of programs, object files and libraries
.TP
.B size
print the sizes of programs and object files
.TP
.B strip
remove the symbol table from programs
.TP
.B clear
clear the terminal screen
.TP
.B man
print pages of this manual
.PP
With them come the system tools, a package of their own, TOOLS:
.TP 10
.B ls
list the contents of directories, in place of XENIX's own, with the
same names l, lc, lf, lr and lx, and an option \-h for sizes in K and M
.TP
.B df
report free disk space, in place of XENIX's own, in K and M with \-v
and \-h
.TP
.B date
print the date and time, or set the clock, in place of XENIX's own,
which cannot be set past the end of the century
.TP
.B whereis
locate the program, source and manual page of a command
.TP
.B xtop
show the processes using the machine, kept up to date; also called top
.TP
.B hostname
print or set the name of this machine
.TP
.B cursor
make the console's cursor a block or a line
.TP
.B lastlogin
tell when you last logged in
.PP
and the start-up files for new accounts: mkuser gives each one a .cshrc
whose prompt shows the user, the machine and the current directory (~
for the home directory), in color on the console, and a .login and
.profile that say when the account last logged in, and that make the
console's cursor a block if one line in them is uncommented.
.PP
The debugger
.B adb
and the system headers in /usr/include/sys come with the Operating
System, on its disk N5, which the Development System's installation asks
for.
.PP
Section 3 of the manual describes the functions of the libraries; see
.BR intro (3).
.SH "A FIRST PROGRAM"
.nf
	$ cat hello.c
	main()
	{
		printf("hello, world\en");
	}
	$ cc \-o hello hello.c
	$ ./hello
	hello, world
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A command exits with status 0 when it succeeds, and with another
status when it fails; the shell's
.B $?
holds it, and
.B make
stops on it.
.SH SEE ALSO
man(1), intro(3)
.PP
B. W. Kernighan and D. M. Ritchie,
.IR "The C Programming Language" ,
Prentice-Hall, 1978.
.PP
B. W. Kernighan and R. Pike,
.IR "The UNIX Programming Environment" ,
Prentice-Hall, 1984.
.SH HISTORY
The commands follow those of Version 7 AT&T UNIX, System V and 4.2BSD;
the page for each says where it came from.
